{"cases":[{"attack":false,"case":"The target's claim that preferring better-corroborated theories 'smuggles back in induction' is refuted by Popper's own distinction between the logical status of corroboration (a past report) and the pragmatic rationality of action. Popper argues that while corroboration does not increase probability (induction), it is rational to prefer the theory that has withstood the severest tests because it offers the best chance of falsification if false, thereby maximizing the severity of future testing. This preference is a methodological rule for critical inquiry, not an inductive assumption that the future will resemble the past. The target fails to engage with this specific non-inductive justification for action, instead asserting a contradiction that Popper explicitly addressed by separating logical probability from pragmatic decision-making.","target":"05a234fe135a740bf7b9314c748309191217a817fa03acee2e0de2f9ef249b16"}]}